<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/732">732</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi was the Umayyad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> in 721 and again from 730. In 732 he led a large raid across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and advanced towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, but was defeated and killed by the Frankish leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>). The battle marked the limit of Muslim expansion into western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi" width="184" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles de Steuben · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdul_Rahman_Al_Ghafiqi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah al-Ghafiqi belonged to the Arab Tihamite tribe of Ghafiq. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, and then to the part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> that is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, Tunisia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, where he became acquainted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-nusayr">Musa ibn Nusayr</a> and his son Abd al-Aziz, governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He took part in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 721, where Al Samh ibn Malik was killed by the forces of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo of Aquitaine</a>. After the defeat he fled south with other commanders and took over the command of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Several commanders followed one another after 726, none for long. In 730 the Caliph Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik appointed him wali of al-Andalus, and David Levering Lewis describes him as intelligent, eloquent and an accomplished administrator.</p><p>In 731 he put down a rebellion by Uthman ibn Naissa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> deputy governor of Catalunya, who had allied himself with Odo; Uthman was killed, by some accounts by his own hand. The wali then assembled troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, called for recruits from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> with an army composed chiefly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. He defeated Odo outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, sacked the city, and beat a second army of Odo at the Battle of the River Garonne, where Odo's forces faced the Umayyad cavalry in open battle and were destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Odo fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, who was campaigning on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and Charles force-marched a seasoned infantry towards Aquitaine, adding militia to form an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and Germans of about 30,000. Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, in 732, he chose a high, wooded plain, where the trees and hills hindered cavalry. For seven days the armies skirmished. Charles formed his men in a large square, and most historians agree he was badly outnumbered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> held all day against repeated charges of the Umayyad heavy cavalry.</p><p>According to Lewis the Umayyad soldiers were accompanied by their families. When a large contingent broke off to save its plundered camp, Abd al-Rahman was left exposed and was killed trying to rally his men, on 10 October 732. The surviving generals could not agree on a commander, and ethnic and tribal rivalries led to a retreat of an army that was still dangerous.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah al-Ghafiqi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The mayors of the palace restored it by force: Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad army under <b>Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</b> between Poitiers and Tours, usually dated 732, and his son Pippin the Short subdued Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Frankish forces under Charles Martel defeated an Arab and Berber army led by <b>Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</b> between Tours and Poitiers; the traditional date is 10 October.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-10">10 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Raids pushed into Gaul until <b>Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</b> was defeated near Poitiers in 732.</blockquote><a href="/state/al-andalus-umayyad-province">Al-Andalus (Umayyad province)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.5,40.86&amp;year=722" aria-label="Hejaz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5847 5514h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5847 5514h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.5,40.86&amp;year=722">What was here in 722 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
